1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
PDPA Cross-Border Rules and Outsourced Document Scanning. What changed in 2026 for Singapore organisations

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:19,699
Singapore's Transfer Limitation Obligation requires that before personal data leaves Singapore, the sending organisation ensures the overseas recipient protects it to a standard comparable to the PDPA.

3
00:00:19,699 --> 00:00:27,500
The sender stays accountable afterwards. Scanning, OCR and storage that all stay in Singapore avoid the cross-border question for that step.

4
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:33,094
The Personal Data Protection (Amendment) Regulations 2026 took effect on 2 March 2026.

5
00:00:33,094 --> 00:00:39,718
They amended regulation 12(2) of the PDP Regulations 2021, expanding the certification mechanisms organisations can rely on.

6
00:00:39,718 --> 00:00:47,250
The PDPC's updated Guide to Cross-Border Data Transfers (14 April 2026) recognises Global CBPR for controllers and Global PRP for processors.

7
00:00:47,250 --> 00:00:53,500
These sit alongside existing contracts and Binding Corporate Rules. Source: PDPC / IMDA, as summarised in the article

8
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:00,195
An offshore scanning bureau handling records or images is a transfer of personal data overseas.

9
00:01:00,195 --> 00:01:05,410
A cloud document platform with servers outside Singapore is the same category of event.

10
00:01:05,410 --> 00:01:12,783
Overseas-hosted OCR, AI indexing or classification is a transfer in its own right, even if final files return to Singapore.

11
00:01:12,783 --> 00:01:16,500
The sending organisation, not the vendor, remains accountable.

12
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:25,222
1. Location of processing: documents, equipment and output in Singapore?. 2. OCR and AI routing: any overseas engine, even transiently?. 3.

13
00:01:25,222 --> 00:01:34,500
Certification: CBPR, PRP or contract clauses?. 4. Subcontractor chain: undisclosed overseas storage or backup?. 5. Audit evidence: documentation, not verbal assurance.

14
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:44,503
Does scanning with a Singapore vendor avoid this? For the scanning step, yes, if records, equipment and digital files never leave Singapore.

15
00:01:44,503 --> 00:01:50,162
It still applies to later steps such as an overseas cloud backup. What are CBPR and PRP?

16
00:01:50,162 --> 00:01:55,500
CBPR is a certification for data controllers; PRP is the equivalent for processors.

17
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:59,447
